  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Latest surface analysis shows a large and \nstrengthening low pressure system lifting towards the Mid-\nAtlantic coast this afternoon. Overall, very little change in \nforecast thinking. Wrap around moisture could bring light rain\nat times to the Outer Banks, though best rain chances will\nremain offshore. Below normal temps will continue tonight into \nSat. \n\nBy far, the greatest impacts with this Nor'easter remain across\nthe Outer Banks, with coastal flooding, dangerous surf, beach\nerosion and rip currents. Winds have trended downward and will \nplan to cancel the Wind Advisory, though will still remain \nbreezy tonight. Wave runup continues to be the main concern with\nthe threat for minor to locally moderate and significant ocean \noverwash along portions of Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands. Seas \nremain at 10-16 ft about 13-14 seconds. While seas have peaked, \nthey will be slow to subside, so expect another round of ocean \noverwash with this evenings high tide (around 7 pm) and likely \nagain Sat morning (around 730 AM). Extended CF Warnings and \nAdvisories into Sat morning. Portions of NC 12 on Hatteras and \nOcracoke Island remain closed. A High Surf Advisory Remains in \neffect through Saturday evening. Outer Banks soundside water \nlevels continue to recede with grad weakening northerly winds, \nthough could still see minor inundation into tonight. Minor \ninundation may continue for Downeast Carteret into tonight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...As the low shifts to New England and eventually\nweakens, sfc high and ridging aloft builds into ENC early next \nweek. This will bring lighter winds, sunshine, and warmer \nweather back to the region. Of note, despite the warming \nweather, dewpoints are forecast to remain in the 50s into \nmidweek next week, making for more comfortable conditions \ndespite highs warming up into the mid to upper 80s. Slightly \nhigher dewpoints into the 60s look to return closer to the end \nof next week.",
